\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces The intensity noise of the light at the AS port for the A+ detector. This was obtained by deconvolving the detectors sensitivity curve with the function shown in Figure \ref  {fig:bhdforligochapterdetectorrepsonse}. At frequencies above 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kHz the noise is flat as it is dominated by shot noise. At lower frequencies the noise is increased relative to shot noise due to radiation pressure, coating thermal noise and other noise sources. The spike in the spectrum at 500Hz represents the presence of suspension modes (\leavevmode {\color {red}actually find out what this is}). \relax }}{34}{figure.caption.37}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterlightnoise}{{5.4}{34}{The intensity noise of the light at the AS port for the A+ detector. This was obtained by deconvolving the detectors sensitivity curve with the function shown in Figure \ref {fig:bhdforligochapterdetectorrepsonse}. At frequencies above 1\,kHz the noise is flat as it is dominated by shot noise. At lower frequencies the noise is increased relative to shot noise due to radiation pressure, coating thermal noise and other noise sources. The spike in the spectrum at 500Hz represents the presence of suspension modes (\textcolor {red}{actually find out what this is}). \relax }{figure.caption.37}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces A sketch of the LO and signal beam paths. The LO beam path is shown in blue, and the signal beam is shown in red. A dashed arrow indicates the direction that the recombining beam splitter, BHDBS2, may move in. If BHDBS2 moves in this way, the phase of the signal beam has increased while the phase of the LO beam has decreased at the point where the signal and LO beams combine. This means that the recombination beam splitter motion needs to be counted twice in calculations of the phase noise requirement.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.43}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterbhddoublecounted}{{5.5}{35}{A sketch of the LO and signal beam paths. The LO beam path is shown in blue, and the signal beam is shown in red. A dashed arrow indicates the direction that the recombining beam splitter, BHDBS2, may move in. If BHDBS2 moves in this way, the phase of the signal beam has increased while the phase of the LO beam has decreased at the point where the signal and LO beams combine. This means that the recombination beam splitter motion needs to be counted twice in calculations of the phase noise requirement.\relax }{figure.caption.43}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces The phase noise between the LO and the signal beams causes noise. The sensitivity curve for A+ sets the limit on how much path length noise is acceptable between the AS and LO beams. The blue line shows sensitivity curve is shown in units of path length noise between the AS and LO beams, and the orange line shows the requirement of HRTS mirror motion. The table motion was modelled as $1/f$ noise.\relax }}{36}{figure.caption.44}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterhrtsrequirement}{{5.6}{36}{The phase noise between the LO and the signal beams causes noise. The sensitivity curve for A+ sets the limit on how much path length noise is acceptable between the AS and LO beams. The blue line shows sensitivity curve is shown in units of path length noise between the AS and LO beams, and the orange line shows the requirement of HRTS mirror motion. The table motion was modelled as $1/f$ noise.\relax }{figure.caption.44}{}}
